	affiliations = {Nanjing Medical University, Nanjing, China; Changhai Hospital, Department of Urology, Shanghai, China; Faculty of Health Sciences, Cancer Centre and Institute of Translational Medicine, Taipa, Macao; TU Wien, Institute of Logic and Computation, Vienna, Austria; Zhujiang Hospital, Southern Medical University, Department of Oncology, Guangzhou, China; Shanghai General Hospital, Department of Urology, Shanghai, China; Zhuhai People's Hospital, Department of Joint Surgery and Sports Medicine, Zhuhai, China; Nanfang Hospital, Southern Medical University, Department of Oncology, Guangzhou, China; South China University of Technology, Comprehensive Cancer Center, Guangzhou, China; Wenzhou Medical University, Department of Hepatobiliary Surgery, Wenzhou, China; Qingdao University, Department of Urology, Qingdao, China; University of Electronic Science and Technology of China, Department of Radiology, Chengdu, China; The University of Hong Kong Li Ka Shing Faculty of Medicine, Hong Kong, Hong Kong; Monash University, Melbourne, Australia; Suzhou Industrial Park Monash Research Institute of Science and Technology, Suzhou, China; Anhui Medical University, College and Hospital of Stomatology, Hefei, China; Sun Yat-Sen University Cancer Center, Department of Urology, Guangzhou, China; First Affiliated Hospital of Harbin Medical University, Department of Urology, Harbin, China; Xiangya Hospital Central South University, Department of Neurosurgery, Changsha, China; Xiangya Hospital Central South University, National Clinical Research Center for Geriatric Disorders, Changsha, China; Fudan University, Department of Interventional Radiology, Shanghai, China},
	abstract = {Background: As clinical trials scale up and grow more complex, researchers are facing mounting challenges, including inefficient participant recruitment, complex data management, and limited risk monitoring. These issues not only increase the workload for clinical researchers but also compromise trial reliability and safety, potentially elevating the risk of trial failure. Large language models (LLMs), as an emerging technology in natural language processing (NLP), exhibit notable advantages across various tasks, such as information extraction and relation classification. Main text: With domain-specific pre-training and fine-tuning, LLMs present promising potential in clinical trial tasks such as automated patient-trial matching and the extraction and processing of trial data, which are anticipated to reduce time and financial costs. Additionally, they offer valuable insights for scientific rationale, medical decision-making, and trial endpoint prediction. In this context, an increasing number of studies have begun to explore the applications of LLMs in the design and conduct of clinical trials. Conclusion: This paper provides a review of LLM applications in clinical trials with an emphasis on real-world integration. Comparative advantages over traditional NLP models, technical limitations, and future implementation challenges are also discussed. This narrative review aims to highlight the potential of LLMs in clinical trial workflows and clarify key challenges and future directions. © 2025 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.},
	author_keywords = {Clinical data management; Clinical trials; Large language models; LLMs; Natural language processing},
